## Server-authoritative mode

Set `statusUrl`, `expiredRedirectUrl` (a non-destructive login page), and
`sessionKey` (an opaque identifier for the currently rendered login) to opt in.
Without `statusUrl`, the original timeout-dialog.js behavior remains available.

Both status and keep-alive endpoints receive GET parameters `format=json` and
`sessionKey`. Return uncached JSON containing numeric Unix timestamps
`serverTime` and `expiresAt`, and a string `sessionKey`. Status must enforce expiry
without renewing it. Keep-alive must verify authentication and the supplied login
identifier before renewing. Return HTTP 401 for expiry and HTTP 409 when a renewal
belongs to another login. Do not redirect these endpoints to HTML login pages.

The browser checks at warning/expiry boundaries and on focus/resume. localStorage
notifications accelerate cross-tab synchronization; correctness does not depend on
storage being available. Automatic expiry uses `expiredRedirectUrl` without
calling logout. Explicit sign-out still uses `logoutUrl` / `logoutRedirectUrl`.
Network failures leave a retryable dialog and do not claim renewal or log out.
`networkErrorMessage` can be localized by the application. In this mode the server
controls renewal, so legacy `timeout` and `restartOnYes` do not control expiry.

Run `node widgets/timeout/tests/session-timeout.test.js` from the extension root.